","Let $v_0$ be the initial velocity of the particle, which is $20 \, \mathrm{m/s}$.
Let $v$ be the velocity of the particle at time $t$.
Let $h$ be the height of the particle at time $10$.
The acceleration is given by $a = -9.8 \, \mathrmmathrm{m/s}^2$.
The equation for the velocity is $v = v_0 + at$, so $v = 20 - 9.8t$.
The equation for the position is $h = u_0t + \frac{1}{2}at^2$, where $u_0$ is the initial initial position. Since the initial position is the ground, $u_0 = 0$. So $h = 0 + \frac{1}{2}(-9.8)t^2 = -4.9t^2$.
The maximum height is reached when the velocity is zero.
So, $0 = 20 - 9.8t$, which implies $t = \frac{20}{9.8} = \frac{10}{4.9} = \frac{100}{49} \approx 2.04 \, \mathrm{s}$.
