","Let the surface be given by the equation $F(x, y, z) = x^2 + y^2 + 9z^2 - 56 = 0$.
We want to find the equation of the normal line to the surface at the point $(4, 2, -2)$.
First, we find the gradient of the function $F(x, y, z)$.
$$ \nabla F(x, y, z) = \left( \frac{\partial F}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial F}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial \partial F}}{\partial z} \right) = (2x, 2y, 18z) $$
At the point $(4, 2, -2)$, the gradient is
$$ \nabla F(4, 2, -2) = (2(4), 2(2), 18(-2)) = (8, 4, -36) $$
The normal vector to the surface at the point $(4, 2, -2)$ is $\langle 8, 4, -36 \)$.
The equation of the normal line is given by
$$ \vec{x} = \langle 4, 2, -2 \right) + t \langle 8, 4, -36 \right) $$
$$ \vec{x} = \langle 4 + 8t, 2 + 4t, -2 - 36t \right) $$
The equation of the normal line is
$$ x = 4 + 8t $$
$$ y = 2 + 4t $$
$$ z = -2 - 36t $$
We can express the equation of the normal line as
$$ \left( \frac{x-4}{8}, \frac{y-2}{4}, \frac{z+2}{-36} \right) $$
Alternatively, we can write the equation of the normal line as
$$ \left( x-4 \right) \cdot 8 + \left( y-2 \right) \cdot 4 + \left( z+2 \right) \cdot (-36) = 0 $$
$$ 8(x-4) + 4(y-2) - 36(z+2) = 0 $$
$$ 8x - 32 + 4y - 8 - 36z - 72 = 0 $$
$$ 8x + 4y - 36z - 10 + (-36) = 0 $$
$$ 8x + 4y - 36z - 112 = 0 $$
$$ 2x + y - 9z - 28 = 0 $$
The equation of the normal line is
$$ x = 4 + 8t, \quad y = 2 + 4t, \quad z = -2 - 36t $$

