","(1) We have
$$ \frac{\cos (180 ^circ + \alpha) \cdot \sin (\alpha + 360 ^circ)}{\sin (-\alpha - 180 ^circ) \cdot \cos (-180 ^circ - \alpha)} $$
We know that $\cos(180^\circ + \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$, $\sin(\alpha + 360^\circ) = \sin \alpha$, $\sin(-\alpha - 180^\circ) = \sin(\alpha + 180^\degree) = -\sin \alpha$, and $\cos(-180^\circ - \alpha) = \cos(\alpha + 180^\circ) = -\cos \alpha$.
Therefore,
$$ \frac{\cos (180 ^circ + \alpha) \cdot \sin (\alpha + 360 ^circ)}{\sin (-\alpha - 110 ^circ) \cdot \cos (-180 ^circ - \alpha)} = \frac{(-\cos \alpha) \cdot (\sin \alpha)}{(-\sin \alpha) \cdot (-\cos \alpha)} = \frac{- \cos \alpha \cdot \sin \alpha}{\cos \alpha \cdot \alpha} = -1 $$

(2) We are given $\tan \alpha = -\frac{3}{4}$. We want to find
$$ \frac{\cos \left(\frac{\pi}{2} + \alpha\right) \cdot \sin (-\pi - \alpha)}{\cos \left(\frac{11 \pi}{2} - \alpha\right) \cdot \sin \left(\frac{11 \pi}{2} + \alpha\right)} $$
We have $\cos(\frac{\pi}{2} + \alpha) = -\sin \alpha$, $\sin(-\pi - \alpha) = \sin(\pi + \alpha) = -\sin \alpha$.
Also, $\frac{11 \pi}{2} = \frac{10 \pi}{2} + \frac{\pi}{2} = 5\pi + \frac{\pi}{2} = 4\pi + \frac{\pi}{2} + \pi = 2\pi + \frac{\pi}{2} + \pi$.
So $\cos\left(\frac{11 \pi}{2} - \alpha\right) = \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2} - \alpha\right) = \cos(\frac{\pi}{2} - \alpha) = \sin \alpha$.
And $\sin\left(\frac{11 \pi}{2} + \alpha\right) = \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{2} + \alpha\right) = \cos \alpha$.
Therefore,
$$ \frac{\cos \left(\frac{\pi}{2} + \alpha\right) \cdot \sin (-\pi - \alpha)}{\cos \left(\frac{11 \pi}{2} - \alpha\right) \cdot \sin \left(\frac{11 \pi}{2} + \alpha\right)} = \frac{(-\sin \alpha) (-\sin \alpha)}{\cos \left(\frac{\pi}{2} - \alpha\right) \cdot \cos \alpha} = \frac{\sin^2 \alpha}{\sin \alpha \cdot \cos \alpha} = \frac{\sin \alpha}{\cos \alpha} = \tan \alpha = -\frac{3}{4} $$

