3. Arrange the results into a \(50 \times 11\) matrix where each row is a realization.  

(b) Plotting 8 realizations will show that the process is nonstationary because the variance grows with time. This is evident as the spread of the realizations increases as \(k\) increases.  

(c) The average squares of the realizations at each time step \(k\) estimate \(E[X_k^2]\). Since \(X_k\) is the sum of \(k\) independent \(N(0, 1)\) variables, \(E[X_k^2] = k\). Thus, the plot of average squares versus \(k\) should be a straight line with slope 1, confirming the theoretical variance growth of the Wiener process.  

