","Let $I(t) = \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} + 4$.
We want to find $\lim_{t \to \infty} I(t)$.
We have
$$ \lim_{t \to \infty} I(t) = \lim_{t \to \infty} \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} + 4 $$
Since $e^{-t/2} \to 0$ when $t \to \infty$, we can consider the term $\left(\frac{11}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2}$.
Let $f(t) = \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2}$.
We can write $f(t) = \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right} e^{-t/2}$.
We can rewrite $\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t$ as
$$ \frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t = \sqrt{\frac{1}{3} + 1} \left(\frac{\frac{1}{3}}{\sqrt{\frac{1}{3} + 1}} \sin t + \frac{1}{\sqrt{\frac{1}{3} + 1}} \cos t\right) $$
$$ = \sqrt{\frac{4}{3}} \left(\frac{1}{3\sqrt{\frac{4}{3}}} \sin t + \frac{1}{\sqrt{\frac{4}{3}}} \cos t\right) = \frac{2\sqrt{3}}{3} \left(\frac{1}{2\sqrt{3}} \sin t + \frac{1}{2} \cos t\right) $$
Let $\alpha$ be an angle such that $\cos \alpha = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{3}}$ and $\sin \alpha = \frac{1}{2}$.
Then $\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t = \frac{2\sqrt{3}}{3} (\cos \alpha \sin t + \sin \alpha \cos t) = \frac{2\sqrt{3}}{3} \sin(t+\alpha)$.
So
$$ \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} = \frac{2\sqrt{3}}{3} \sin(t+\alpha) e^{-t/2} $$
Since $\left| \sin(t+\alpha) \right| \le 1$, we have
$$ \left| \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} \right| \leq \frac{2\sqrt{3}}{3} e^{-t/2} \to 0 \text{ when $t \to \infty$} $$
Therefore, $\lim_{t \to \infty} \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t2} = 0$.
